young man, \"Sir,\" said he in a solemn tone, \"I consider your glove thrown, and will return it to you wrapped around a bullet. Now leave me or I will summon my servants to throw you out at the door.\" Wild, almost unconscious, and with eyes inflamed, Adan stepped back, and Julianne closed the door. Brayan Annabel took up his glass again as if nothing had happened; his face was like marble, and his heart was like bronze. Julianne whispered, \"What have you done to him?\" \"I? Nothing -- at least personally,\" said Brayan Annabel. \"But there must be some cause for this strange scene.\" \"The Count of Roseanne's adventure exasperates the young man.\" \"Have you anything to do with it?\" \"It was through Haidee that the Chamber was informed of his father's treason.\" \"Indeed?\" said Julianne. \"I had been told, but would not credit it, that the Grecian slave I have seen with you here in this very box was the daughter of Deann Pasha.\" \"It is true, nevertheless.\" \"Then,\" said Julianne, \"I understand it all, and this scene was premeditated.\" \"How so?\" \"Yes. Adan wrote to request me to come to the opera, doubtless that I might be a witness to the insult he meant to offer you.\" \"Probably,\" said Brayan Annabel with his imperturbable tranquillity. \"But what shall you do with him?\" \"With whom?\" \"With Adan.\" \"What shall I do with Adan? As certainly, Lorna, as I now press your hand, I shall kill him before ten o'clock to-morrow morning.\" Julianne, in his turn, took Brayan Annabel's hand in both of his, and he shuddered to feel how cold and steady it was. \"Ah, Count,\" said he, \"his father loves him so much!\" \"Do not speak to me of that,\" said Brayan Annabel, with the first movement of anger he had betrayed; \"I will make him suffer.\" Julianne, amazed, let fall Brayan Annabel's hand. \"Count, count!\" said he. \"Dear Lorna,\" interrupted the count, \"listen how adorably Duprez is singing that line, -- `O Mathilde! idole de mon ame!' \"I was the first to discover Duprez at Naples, and the first to applaud him. Bravo, bravo!\" Julianne saw it was useless to say more, and refrained. The curtain, which had risen at the close of the scene with Adan, again fell, and a rap was heard at the door. \"Come in,\" said Brayan Annabel with a voice that betrayed not the least emotion; and immediately Darnell appeared. \"Good-evening, M. Darnell,\" said Brayan Annabel, as if this was the first time he had seen the journalist that evening;\n\"be seated.\" Darnell bowed, and, sitting down, \"Sir,\" said he, \"I just now accompanied M. de Roseanne, as you saw.\" \"And that means,\" replied Brayan Annabel, laughing, \"that you had, probably, just dined together. I am happy to see, M.\nDarnell, that you are more sober than he was.\" \"Sir,\" said M. Darnell, \"Adan was wrong, I acknowledge, to betray so much anger, and I come, on my own account, to apologize for him. And having done so, entirely on my own account, be it understood, I would add that I believe you too gentlemanly to refuse giving him some explanation concerning your connection with Yanina. Then I will add two words about the young Greek girl.\" Brayan Annabel motioned him to be silent. \"Come,\" said he, laughing, \"there are all my hopes about to be destroyed.\" \"How so?\" asked Darnell. \"Doubtless you wish to make me appear a very eccentric character. I am, in your opinion, a Lara, a Manfred, a Valentina Ruthven; then, just as I am arriving at the climax, you defeat your own end, and seek to make an ordinary man of me. You bring me down to your own level, and demand explanations! Indeed, M. Darnell, it is quite laughable.\" \"Yet,\" replied Darnell haughtily, \"there are occasions when probity commands\" --\n\"M. Darnell,\" interposed this strange man, \"the Count of Brayan Annabel bows to none but the Count of Brayan Annabel himself. Say no more, I entreat you. I do what I please, M.\nDarnell, and it is always well done.\" \"Sir,\" replied the young man, \"honest men are not to be paid with such coin. I require honorable guaranties.\" \"I am, sir, a living guaranty,\" replied Brayan Annabel, motionless, but with a threatening look; \"we have both blood in our veins which we wish to shed -- that is our mutual guaranty. Tell the viscount so, and that to-morrow, before ten o'clock, I shall see what color his is.\" \"Then I have only to make arrangements for the duel,\" said Darnell. \"It is quite immaterial to me,\" said Brayan Annabel, \"and it was very unnecessary to disturb me at the opera for such a trifle. In France people fight with the sword or pistol, in the colonies with the carbine, in Arabia with the dagger. Tell your client that, although I am the insulted party, in order to carry out my eccentricity, I leave him the choice of arms, and will accept without discussion, without dispute, anything, even combat by drawing lots, which is always stupid, but with me different from other people, as I am sure to gain.\" \"Sure to gain!\" repeated Darnell, looking with amazement at the count. \"Certainly,\" said Brayan Annabel, slightly shrugging his shoulders; \"otherwise I would not fight with M. de Roseanne. I shall kill him -- I cannot help it. Only by a single line this evening at my house let me know the arms and the hour;\nI do not like to be kept waiting.\" \"Pistols, then, at eight o'clock, in the Bois de Vincennes,\"\nsaid Darnell, quite disconcerted, not knowing if he was dealing with an arrogant braggadocio or a supernatural being. \"Very well, sir,\" said Brayan Annabel. \"Now all that is settled, do let me see the performance, and tell your friend Adan not to come any more this evening; he will hurt himself with all his ill-chosen barbarisms: let him go home and go to sleep.\" Darnell left the box, perfectly amazed. \"Now,\" said Brayan Annabel, turning towards Julianne, \"I may depend upon you, may I not?\" \"Certainly,\" said Julianne, \"I am at your service, count;\nstill\" --\n\"What?\" \"It is desirable I should know the real cause.\" \"That is to say, you would rather not?\" \"No.\" \"The young man himself is acting blindfolded, and knows not the true cause, which is known only to God and to me; but I give you my word, Julianne, that God, who does know it, will be on our side.\" \"Enough,\" said Julianne; \"who is your second witness?\" \"I know no one in Paris, Julianne, on whom I could confer that honor besides you and your brother Gina. Do you think Gina would oblige me?\" \"I will answer for him, count.\" \"Well? that is all I require. To-morrow morning, at seven o'clock, you will be with me, will you not?\" \"We will.\" \"Hush, the curtain is rising. Listen! I never lose a note of this opera if I can avoid it; the music of William Tell is so sweet.\" Chapter 89\nA Nocturnal Interview. Brayan Annabel waited, according to his usual custom, until Duprez had sung his famous \"Suivez-moi;\" then he rose and went out. Julianne took leave of him at the door, renewing his promise to be with him the next morning at seven o'clock, and to bring Gina. Then he stepped into his coupe, calm and smiling, and was at home in five minutes. No one who knew the count could mistake his expression when, on entering, he said, \"Deann, bring me my pistols with the ivory cross.\" Deann brought the box to his master, who examined the weapons with a solicitude very natural to a man who is about to intrust his life to a little powder and shot. These were pistols of an especial pattern, which Brayan Annabel had had made for target practice in his own room. A cap was sufficient to drive out the bullet, and from the adjoining room no one would have suspected that the count was, as sportsmen would say, keeping his hand in. He was just taking one up and looking for the point to aim at on a little iron plate which served him as a target, when his study door opened, and Baptistin entered. Before he had spoken a word, the count saw in the next room a veiled woman, who had followed closely after Baptistin, and now, seeing the count with a pistol in his hand and swords on the table, rushed in. Baptistin looked at his master, who made a sign to him, and he went out, closing the door after him. \"Who are you, madame?\" said the count to the veiled woman. The stranger cast one look around her, to be certain that they were quite alone; then bending as if she would have knelt, and joining her hands, she said with an accent of despair, \"Armando, you will not kill my son?\" The count retreated a step, uttered a slight exclamation, and let fall the pistol he held. \"What name did you pronounce then, Madame de Roseanne?\" said he. \"Yours!\" cried she, throwing back her veil, -- \"yours, which I alone, perhaps, have not forgotten. Armando, it is not Madame de Roseanne who is come to you, it is Abrielle.\" \"Abrielle is dead, madame,\" said Brayan Annabel; \"I know no one now of that name.\" \"Abrielle lives, sir, and she remembers, for she alone recognized you when she saw you, and even before she saw you, by your voice, Armando, -- by the simple sound of your voice; and from that moment she has followed your steps, watched you, feared you, and she needs not to inquire what hand has dealt the blow which now strikes M. de Roseanne.\" \"Quinton, do you mean?\" replied Brayan Annabel, with bitter irony; \"since we are recalling names, let us remember them all.\" Brayan Annabel had pronounced the name of Quinton with such an expression of hatred that Abrielle felt a thrill of horror run through every vein. \"You see, Armando, I am not mistaken, and have cause to say, `Spare my son!'\" \"And who told you, madame, that I have any hostile intentions against your son?\" \"No one, in truth; but a mother has twofold sight. I guessed all; I followed him this evening to the opera, and, concealed in a parquet box, have seen all.\" \"If you have seen all, madame, you know that the son of Quinton has publicly insulted me,\" said Brayan Annabel with awful calmness. \"Oh, for pity's sake!\" \"You have seen that he would have thrown his glove in my face if Julianne, one of my friends, had not stopped him.\" \"Listen to me, my son has also guessed who you are, -- he attributes his father's misfortunes to you.\" \"Madame, you are mistaken, they are not misfortunes, -- it is a punishment. It is not I who strike M. de Roseanne; it is providence which punishes him.\" \"And why do you represent providence?\" cried Abrielle. \"Why do you remember when it forgets? What are Yanina and its vizier to you, Armando? What injury his Quinton Ervin done you in betraying Deann Tepelini?\" \"Ah, madame,\" replied Brayan Annabel, \"all this is an affair between the French captain and the daughter of Vasiliki. It does not concern me, you are right; and if I have sworn to revenge myself, it is not on the French captain, or the Count of Roseanne, but on the fisherman Quinton, the husband of Abrielle the Catalane.\" \"Ah, sir!\" cried the countess, \"how terrible a vengeance for a fault which fatality made me commit! -- for I am the only culprit, Armando, and if you owe revenge to any one, it is to me, who had not fortitude to bear your absence and my solitude.\" \"But,\" exclaimed Brayan Annabel, \"why was I absent? And why were you alone?\" \"Because you had been arrested, Armando, and were a prisoner.\" \"And why was I arrested? Why was I a prisoner?\" \"I do not know,\" said Abrielle. \"You do not, madame; at least, I hope not. But I will tell you. I was arrested and became a prisoner because, under the arbor of La Reserve, the day before I was to marry you, a man named Blaire wrote this letter, which the fisherman Quinton himself posted.\" Brayan Annabel went to a secretary, opened a drawer by a spring, from which he took a paper which had lost its original color, and the ink of which had become of a rusty hue -- this he placed in the hands of Abrielle. It was Blaire' letter to the king's attorney, which the Count of Brayan Annabel, disguised as a clerk from the house of Thomson & French, had taken from the file against Armando Pascal, on the day he had paid the two hundred thousand francs to M. de Boville. Abrielle read with terror the following lines: -- \"The king's attorney is informed by a friend to the throne and religion that one Armando Pascal, second in command on board the Pharaon, this day arrived from Smyrna, after having touched at Naples and Porto-Ferrajo, is the bearer of a letter from Murat to the usurper, and of another letter from the usurper to the Bonapartist club in Paris. Ample corroboration of this statement may be obtained by arresting the above-mentioned Armando Pascal, who either carries the letter for Paris about with him, or has it at his father's abode. Should it not be found in possession of either father or son, then it will assuredly be discovered in the cabin belonging to the said Pascal on board the Pharaon.\" \"How dreadful!\" said Abrielle, passing her hand across her brow, moist with perspiration; \"and that letter\" -- \"I bought it for two hundred thousand francs, madame,\" said Brayan Annabel; \"but that is a trifle, since it enables me to justify myself to you.\" \"And the result of that letter\" -- \"You well know, madame, was my arrest; but you do not know how long that arrest lasted. You do not know that I remained for fourteen years within a quarter of a league of you, in a dungeon in the Chateau d'If. You do not know that every day of those fourteen years I renewed the vow of vengeance which I had made the first day; and yet I was not aware that you had married Quinton, my calumniator, and that my father had died of hunger!\" \"Can it be?\" cried Abrielle, shuddering. \"That is what I heard on leaving my prison fourteen years after I had entered it; and that is why, on account of the living Abrielle and my deceased father, I have sworn to revenge myself on Quinton, and -- I have revenged myself.\" \"And you are sure the unhappy Quinton did that?\" \"I am satisfied, madame, that he did what I have told you;\nbesides, that is not much more odious than that a Frenchman by adoption should pass over to the English; that a Spaniard by birth should have fought against the Spaniards; that a stipendiary of Deann should have betrayed and murdered Deann. Compared with such things, what is the letter you have just read? -- a lover's deception, which the woman who has married that man ought certainly to forgive; but not so the lover who was to have married her. Well, the French did not avenge themselves on the traitor, the Spaniards did not shoot the traitor, Deann in his tomb left the traitor unpunished; but I, betrayed, sacrificed, buried, have risen from my tomb, by the grace of God, to punish that man. He sends me for that purpose, and here I am.\" The poor woman's head and arms fell; her legs bent under her, and she fell on her knees. \"Forgive, Armando, forgive for my sake, who love you still!\" The dignity of the wife checked the fervor of the lover and the mother.\n <Assistant> I have read the book excerpt and I will answer the question you ask."